WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.915
This is RUNSAFE, by Marc Donovici, an IT auditor in Strasbourg.

2
00:00:05.865 --> 00:00:09.101
RUNSAFE is design software that uses mathematical

3
00:00:09.101 --> 00:00:11.678
proof to give knitwear a damage rating:

4
00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:13.964
how far one dropped stitch can run.

5
00:00:13.964 --> 00:00:18.273
So small faults stay repairable, and garments stay in use.

6
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.933
Your phone has a water rating.

7
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:25.500
Your sunscreen has an SPF.

8
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.111
Your sweater has nothing:

9
00:00:27.111 --> 00:00:30.591
no one can tell you how far a dropped stitch will run.

10
00:00:30.591 --> 00:00:33.467
Here are two sections of the same sleeve.

11
00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:36.984
In plain knitting, the ladder runs right to the edge.

12
00:00:36.984 --> 00:00:41.841
With RUNSAFE, it stops within nine loops, and that limit is proved.

13
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.947
Marc's job is checking whether claims hold up.

14
00:00:48.897 --> 00:00:50.088
In a fine knit top,

15
00:00:50.088 --> 00:00:54.854
a stitch dropped at the elbow can ladder twenty-two centimetres to the cuff,

16
00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:57.336
and an almost intact top is thrown away.

17
00:00:57.336 --> 00:01:02.648
RUNSAFE began with a question: could knitwear carry a rating anyone can check?

18
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.503
The idea is simple.

19
00:01:07.453 --> 00:01:10.650
Every eight rows, RUNSAFE knits a barrier course,

20
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:14.042
where each loop is held by two loops instead of one.

21
00:01:14.042 --> 00:01:17.805
When a ladder reaches it, the second holder stops it.

22
00:01:17.805 --> 00:01:20.578
And the proof doesn't rely on friction.

23
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.801
The hard part is the proof.

24
00:01:27.751 --> 00:01:30.080
Up to nine stitches can drop from

25
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.974
this sleeve in a 33-digit number of ways.

26
00:01:32.974 --> 00:01:34.718
No lab can test them all,

27
00:01:34.718 --> 00:01:37.648
so the worst case is proved for every one.

28
00:01:37.648 --> 00:01:43.599
First, the fabric: one dropped stitch opens at most one segment between barriers;

29
00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:46.685
nine open at most forty-five, at any size.

30
00:01:46.685 --> 00:01:50.439
Second, the machine: the program adds loops of its own,

31
00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:53.169
and the limit holds even when they fail.

32
00:01:53.169 --> 00:02:00.135
Third, the cost: from 155 times the machine passes, down to 1.31.

33
00:02:00.135 --> 00:02:02.536
And it is all checked with Carnegie

34
00:02:02.536 --> 00:02:06.789
Mellon's knitting simulator and over 800,000 exhaustive tests.

35
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.675
So a designer no longer draws barriers.

36
00:02:13.625 --> 00:02:18.149
They state the rating: one dropped stitch within a centimetre,

37
00:02:18.149 --> 00:02:22.453
nine under 450 loops, and at most 35 percent more knitting.

38
00:02:22.453 --> 00:02:27.948
RUNSAFE returns the cheapest proved construction: a barrier every eight rows.

39
00:02:27.948 --> 00:02:31.919
Ask for 300 loops, and nothing fits the budget.

40
00:02:31.919 --> 00:02:36.025
Allow 50 percent, and it switches to staggered barriers,

41
00:02:36.025 --> 00:02:40.643
compiled, verified, and exported as a Shima Seiki machine file.

42
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.115
The rating goes on the care label.

43
00:02:53.065 --> 00:02:56.694
Scan it, and your own phone downloads the knitting program,

44
00:02:56.694 --> 00:02:59.523
rebuilds the loops and checks the proof again.

45
00:02:59.523 --> 00:03:01.953
A brand, a repairer or a buyer can

46
00:03:01.953 --> 00:03:05.240
verify it without taking anyone's word for it.

47
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:11.757
If a stitch drops,

48
00:03:11.757 --> 00:03:16.448
the Studio shows which loops were released and lists the ones to re-form.

49
00:03:16.398 --> 00:03:20.760
How repairers restore barrier loops is one of the first tests.

50
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.535
And the sleeve stays one continuous yarn,

51
00:03:23.535 --> 00:03:26.242
ready to unravel at the end of its life.

52
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.047
Where does it go?

53
00:03:31.997 --> 00:03:34.888
Fine knitwear in smooth yarns first.

54
00:03:34.888 --> 00:03:41.385
Next, tights, the classic ladder: 130 million pairs a year in France alone.

55
00:03:41.385 --> 00:03:45.513
Then, a damage rating in digital product passports.

56
00:03:45.513 --> 00:03:46.877
And the climate case:

57
00:03:46.877 --> 00:03:52.007
the barrier repays its extra knitting once it prevents 1.3 percent of discards,

58
00:03:52.007 --> 00:03:55.930
even if only half of the longer wear replaces a new purchase.

59
00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:05.602
The three biggest challenges are physical validation, repair and adoption.

60
00:04:05.552 --> 00:04:07.582
First, a knitting laboratory,

61
00:04:07.582 --> 00:04:11.503
to test the limit on real fabric after washing and wear.

62
00:04:11.503 --> 00:04:16.605
Second, repair specialists, to show how barrier loops can be restored,

63
00:04:16.605 --> 00:04:17.844
and at what cost.

64
00:04:17.844 --> 00:04:20.652
Third, a brand partner, to judge the look,

65
00:04:20.652 --> 00:04:23.794
the production time and the willingness to pay.

66
00:04:23.794 --> 00:04:25.294
That is the next step:

67
00:04:25.294 --> 00:04:29.042
turning a proved design into knitwear that people keep.
